# Architecture guardrails

Adapt depth to your architecture; these patterns transfer across stacks.

Physical folders and placement conventions → [`folder-structure`](./folder-structure.mdc) · [`../references/engineering/folder-structure.md`](../references/engineering/folder-structure.md). Layer/port semantics for backends → [`clean-ddd-hexagonal`](../skills/clean-ddd-hexagonal/SKILL.md). This rule owns **dependency direction and entrypoints**; folder-structure owns **where files live**; clean-ddd owns **which layer** a unit belongs in.

## Layered / hexagonal layout

```
domain/ — pure business rules, no framework imports
application/ — use cases, ports
infrastructure/ — DB, HTTP clients, queues
presentation/ — controllers, UI, CLI
public-api/ — only legal cross-module import surface
```

## Dependency rule

Dependencies point **inward**. Outer layers implement interfaces defined by inner layers. Cross-module imports go through a published `public-api` (or equivalent), never deep imports into another module's internals.

## Entrypoint pattern (HTTP / jobs / CLI)

```
parse input → validate → authorize (actor + tenant + resource) → one use case → map output
```

Authorization is a **separate step** from "record exists." Wrong scope returns the same error as not found.

## Deferred-decisions ledger

Park intentional incompleteness in a **registry** (not ticket IDs in code):

- Row: what was deferred, why safe, owner, trigger to revisit
- Code marker: `// deferred: DD-### — see <registry>`
- Resolve the row when landing the work

